WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.940
It's being called the toxic haze.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:07.800
For weeks, northern Thailand has been rated
one of the most-polluted regions in the world.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.820
The worst wildfires in years combined with
traditional crop-burning practices are blanketing

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.849
the area in smog.

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:20.600
DW correspondent Charlotte Chelsom-Pill travelled
to Chiang Rai province, where thousands of

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.050
people have been left gasping for air and
tourist numbers have plunged because of the

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.850
pollution.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:33.200
It doesn't take long for a small blaze like
this to turn into a rampant wildfire.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.300
That’s something regional Fire Chief Prathip
Choemsom knows only too well.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.610
This month Chiang Rai in northern Thailand
saw some of the most devastating forest fires

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.190
in years.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.360
And the danger is not over yet.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.399
This is a new fire.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:58.000
The trees are very dry here
Massive areas of land, burnt turned to ash.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.150
And the damage doesn’t end there.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.030
This is what happens when you mix forest fires
with heavy pollution.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.140
It’s being called a toxic haze.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.260
Lingering smoke and noxious fumes, creating
a dangerous cocktail.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:19.710
For weeks Chiang Rai has recorded some of
the worst air quality in the world.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.530
Hitting the most vulnerable the hardest.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.540
85 year old Bunlert Jansangphen has lived
here all his life.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.100
He and his wife say the pollution has never
been this bad.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.720
I couldn’t see clearly.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.140
It made my eyes water.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.240
They were really painful.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.430
I'm afraid the smog could get even worse.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.950
And last even longer.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.689
Pollution-related health problems have surged.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:48.820
Health workers say they’re struggling to
cope.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.409
It’s very bad extremely bad.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:56.119
I wasn’t prepared for this.

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:01.860
Sometimes even I can’t breathe well and
have to get medical help/ No matter what we

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.740
do, it’s not enough.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:11.400
Emergency Services blame the wildfires on
an usually dry summer.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:20.090
There’s fear here that the fires could become
the norm.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.069
And authorities want to be ready.

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:25.680
PTC: this fire team doesn’t want to be caught
off guard again.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.620
They’re building dozens of dams, like this
one across these mountains.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:36.290
They’re aimed at stopping fires from spreading
and holding water to put them out.”

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.840
People here are now just praying for the rainy
season to hit and put an end to this toxic

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.840
nightmare.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.340
For this year at least.

